<Hook>

As is face up on the table. You will draw two more cards without replacement. How does the first draw change the chance that the second is an ace?

</Hook>

<Idea>

### Draw the first branches

A probability tree is a list of branches showing what can happen next. Start with the remaining deck after As is removed. Split the first draw into two branches: hit means an ace, miss means a non-ace. These branches are disjoint and cover every possible first card.

The hit branch removes an ace. Its second draw has one fewer favorable card and one fewer total card. The miss branch removes a non-ace. Its second draw has the same number of favorable aces but one fewer total card. The tree therefore has different second-draw fractions depending on the first result. That difference is dependence: learning one result changes the probability of the next.

### Multiply paths and add alternatives

There are four complete paths: hit then hit, hit then miss, miss then hit, and miss then miss. Multiply along one path because you need both stages to happen in sequence. Add probabilities of different complete paths when they describe alternative ways to achieve your event. You may add these four paths without subtracting overlap because a single two-card deal belongs to exactly one path.

<ProbabilityTree />

To get two aces, use only the hit-then-hit path. To get exactly one ace, add hit-then-miss and miss-then-hit. To get at least one ace, add those two paths and hit-then-hit; alternatively subtract miss-then-miss from one. Expand the tree above and verify that all four path fractions add to one. This is a useful check for a missing branch or an incorrect denominator.

### Compare replacement and removal

The multiplication rule is not “multiply the original fractions every time.” It is “multiply the first probability by the probability of the next event given the earlier result.” If you restored the first card before drawing again, the second fraction would stay unchanged. That is a different experiment, and the shortcut box measures how its two-hit chance differs from drawing without replacement.

Order matters in the tree, but not in the final set of two aces. Counting unordered sets reaches the same probability because every unordered two-card set has exactly two ordered paths. Dividing the favorable and total ordered counts by the same number leaves their ratio unchanged. The notebook uses combinations as a second route to the same answer.

<CourseExamples />

### Keep a branch label and a path label separate

A label on the second stage answers a question about the rows that reached that branch. It is a conditional chance. A full path probability answers how often the experiment reaches that branch and then takes the chosen next step. Multiplying along the path includes both requirements.

For example, the river chance after a miss assumes that miss has already happened. Before the turn is dealt, you cannot use that conditional number as though every run followed the miss branch. You must also account for how often that branch is reached.

Read the tree from left to right once, saying what is known after each card. Then read only the paths satisfying the event. This separates updating the deck from choosing which final outcomes count as success.

<Shortcut />

</Idea>

